lly operates from ports like dover  and ken ryan how can it be that they can  avoid paying workers less than the  minimum wage because they do operate  around british ports so how is that  possible  yeah well what everyone says who looks  at this including the lawyers  is  international maritime law combined with  national laws are incredible it's an  incredibly complex area and up until now  it's been thought that that wasn't  something that they could go  and do what they've done  quite  mischievously is to register their ships  through a cypress flag  and therefore avoid and evade the uh  need to inform anybody about what they  were doing uh as the boss of uh pno  openly admitted to parliament yesterday  the most extraordinary testimonial  to the select committees uh what they  did was know that they were breaking the  law and deliberately set out to break  the law but effectively pay people off  for their silence um we can't have a  situation where uh the minimum wage  exists onshore but as soon as you're  offshore and particularly where you're  within  our economic zone that's 12 miles with  within the  off offshore of the uk and that's the  regular route you take we can't have a  situation where those people aren't  covered by british laws on things like  minimum wage and overseas uh cheap labor  is exploited on those routes um it's not  right it's not fair uh in fairness to  pno uh  fairness i can't think of anything fair  at all about what they've done but what  they have done is actually copied what  irish ferries had already done uh and of  course there's then the danger that  other operators the stena this dfds  might feel that they have to do the same  the package that i'm bringing forward  will make it absolutely clear that would  be unacceptable they won't be able to  operate from british ports if they do  and will require a u-turn on what's  happened uh at pno as well and we'll  enforce that with the law  so specifically you make them pay the  minimum wage and you change the law to  ensure that  correct that's exactly uh the plan it's  quite complex as has been revealed the  last couple of weeks because it's not a  single law it's a combination of  different laws working together okay and  differently can you simplify it for us  then can you simplify it further can you  tell us exactly when you plan on  changing this law and just tell us how  you're going to do it in a very simple  form if you can i'm going to need to i  i'm going to need to make that actual  announcement to parliament first but to  give you an idea there are measures that  we can take uh immediately with regards  to the way that ports operate  instructions to ports some ports are  what are called uh trust ports for  example uh and have a different status  and and we'd have a little bit more  control so the things that we can do  immediately  you know  and are already uh underway there are  things which require what's called  secondary legislation that's legislation  which can again be passed in relatively  quick time  and then there are things which are done  through primary legislation that  normally takes longer but i've already  spoken  to my opposite number uh in the house of  commons uh in order to try and build a  consensus around this and i'm pretty  sure um the members of parliament feel  uh the same about this i think um what's  happened is absolutely disgraceful uh  and we intend to make sure that not only  is that route blocked but actually pno  will have to a u-turn uh on all of this  as well  and when will you be making that  announcement to parliament  next week  and it's a it's a comprehensive uh  package which which contains um seven or  eight different steps that we'll be  taking eight steps actually and when are  you hoping that the new law will come  into force  well as i say there's a series of  different things which interlink and  overlap because we're dealing in part  with international maritime law as well  as for example  national minimum wage law as well as  port law and much else  it essentially  ratchets up with every different step  that we um take so my message to piano  is simple uh their their wheezes are not  going to work we are going to legally  require them to go back on it they might  as well start on that now and if they  haven't got the right leadership they  have to do it at the moment and i think  yesterday we saw through that brazen  breathtaking arrogance uh that they  don't then they will probably need to  think about sourcing that out first  mr shaps can you guarantee that those  800 workers will get to keep their jobs  what is it that you're doing to help  them i don't know that it'll be those  800 workers specifically many of them  have um  have or in the process of accepting uh  what was simply a huge payoff from uh or  a big uh payoff from pno and pno piano  admitted this is what this is an  extraordinary thing about it they said  we know we're breaking the law but don't  worry we're paying them off to not  essentially come after us for breaking  the law um i actually know that um and i  was contacted the same day uh which was  last thursday uh about um about this by  other blue chip bosses and i know  they've gone down and actually snapped  up those employees who you know  hardworking people who spent years in  many cases with piano very attractive in  a very tight labor market where  unemployment is what 3.9  those people have been snapped up so i  suspect they'll probably have to employ  others the whole thing is going to end  up costing piano  a lot more money um because of the way  they've gone about this not only will  they end up paying redundancy i suspect  they'll have to end up paying people to  come and work for them again and  actually they've got a lot of reputation  rebuilding to to do as well um so i  think everything about this the way  they've gone about has been hugely  regrettable uh we won't let the  situation stand as the prime minister  said the dispatch box on wednesday and  and next week we'll have a package of  messages measures which which makes it  absolutely clear that they they can  consider themselves on notice on this  minister just to be clear when did you  become aware of the crisis at piano when  did you first become aware of it i was  actually stood at the dispatch box on  thursday when news started to  come out  about it because i happen to have oral  questions um for completeness i should  say that the night before uh i was  informed by my office that there'll be  another round of redundancies uh at  piano uh but piano have made  redundancies in the normal way in the  past including particularly during  coronavirus and so that in itself  whilst obviously really unfortunate uh  if they'd gone through the normal  consultation process worked with the  workers worked with the unions we  wouldn't be sitting here where we are  today although as i i say actually it  would still not be acceptable for them  to be replacing people uh with uh with  people not being paid the minimum wage  so we still would have a problem if that  were the case  the chief executive yesterday said that  actually you were made aware about this  in november are you saying that he's  lying  well i've sent the note actually from  the meeting in november which was with  the parent company dp world when i was  at world  expo  where they simply said the competition's  very tough the the conditions are very  tight well we knew all that because of  coronavirus uh and and they'll be  looking at the shape of their business  and i've actually sent the formal civil  service notes of that meeting i think we  can all see that what they're trying to  do is distract attention the fact of the  matter is that they needed to give 45  days notice to ministers in fact to the  secretary of state for business uh if  you're making these kind of redundancies  they did not do that they did not  provide the uh notice they are using a  weasel approach by saying well it's  flagged to uh the cyprus flag so  therefore those are the ministers we'd  have to inform by the way they didn't  inform them with 45 days notice either  and they're simply saying we don't care  that we broke the law we did it  deliberately and we're just going to pay  people off to put their silence and none  of that is acceptable i'm afraid so  that's very clear then you're calling  for that chief exec to go and do also as  you've stated here you're going to be  changing the law to protect  those workers and also to ensure that  that loophole which piano exploited is  actually closed minister let's just talk  about some of the main issues that  you're also here to discuss you're here  to talk about the infrastructure of  electric cars and also improvements to  technologies such as the apps and charge  points can you just update us on some of  the changes that you've brought in  yeah i mean we're seeing huge numbers of  cars with plugs on the end electric cars  being sold in this country perhaps one  in four one and five right now  and so the infrastructure to charge them  needs to match it uh we do actually  already have the most rapid charges of  any western country  but we want to go a lot further there  are about 30 000 right now charges  public charges we want that to go up by  10 times to 300 000 today we're  announcing an enormous fund to help that  happen but not just increase the number  of charges also make it easier to charge  anyone who's got an electric car i've  driven one for the last three years  nearly uh knows that it's too hassly if  you turn up at a charging station and  you have to start having membership  cards and uh downloading apps we're  we're actually legislating to make all  the more fast chargers uh use  contactless payment so people just tap  their credit card or tap their their  phone and and they paid for it so a lot  more convenience a lot more uptime  because we're forcing the data to be  shared and 